1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,477
The weather report says: seventy
percent chance of rain tomorrow.

2
00:00:04,877 --> 00:00:08,040
But tomorrow it will either
rain, or it will not.

3
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:10,477
So what does seventy percent even mean?

4
00:00:10,927 --> 00:00:14,847
Diya heard this in the kitchen this
morning, while packing her lunch.

5
00:00:15,197 --> 00:00:20,007
And if rain is seventy percent, why
is no rain exactly thirty percent?

6
00:00:20,507 --> 00:00:24,818
To answer this, we need probability:
events, and three simple rules called

7
00:00:24,818 --> 00:00:25,718
axioms.

8
00:00:26,064 --> 00:00:28,773
First, what does a
probability number mean?

9
00:00:28,773 --> 00:00:33,271
Throw a die again and again, and count
how often you get an even number.

10
00:00:33,671 --> 00:00:36,671
After ten throws, the
fraction jumps around.

11
00:00:36,671 --> 00:00:39,691
After a hundred, it is closer to one half.

12
00:00:39,691 --> 00:00:42,176
After six hundred, it hardly moves.

13
00:00:42,576 --> 00:00:47,248
So a probability tells us the share
of times an event happens, if the same

14
00:00:47,248 --> 00:00:49,609
situation repeats many, many times.

15
00:00:49,609 --> 00:00:54,418
For tomorrow, it means: on days just
like it, rain comes about seven times

16
00:00:54,418 --> 00:00:55,382
out of ten.

17
00:00:56,032 --> 00:00:57,132
Now some words.

18
00:00:57,132 --> 00:01:01,101
All the possible results together
are called the sample space, S.

19
00:01:01,101 --> 00:01:03,133
For a die, S is one to six.

20
00:01:03,533 --> 00:01:06,390
An event is any part of the sample space.

21
00:01:06,390 --> 00:01:10,434
Getting an even number is the
event E: two, four and six.

22
00:01:10,834 --> 00:01:15,039
The event not E is everything
else in S: one, three and five.

23
00:01:15,439 --> 00:01:17,851
Let F be a number more than three.

24
00:01:17,851 --> 00:01:20,779
E or F means at least one of them happens.

25
00:01:20,779 --> 00:01:23,181
E and F means both happen together.

26
00:01:23,581 --> 00:01:28,200
If two events can never happen together,
they are called mutually exclusive.

27
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:30,600
Like even and odd on a single throw.

28
00:01:31,249 --> 00:01:32,767
Now the three rules.

29
00:01:32,767 --> 00:01:35,639
NCERT calls them the
axioms of probability.

30
00:01:35,639 --> 00:01:39,999
An axiom is a rule we agree on,
and build everything else from.

31
00:01:40,399 --> 00:01:41,299
Rule one.

32
00:01:41,254 --> 00:01:45,230
For any event E, the probability
of E is never negative.

33
00:01:45,980 --> 00:01:46,880
Rule two.

34
00:01:46,749 --> 00:01:49,790
The probability of the
whole sample space is one.

35
00:01:49,790 --> 00:01:51,226
Something must happen.

36
00:01:51,976 --> 00:01:52,956
Rule three.

37
00:01:52,956 --> 00:01:58,343
If E and F are mutually exclusive, the
probability of E or F is the probability

38
00:01:58,343 --> 00:02:00,866
of E, plus the probability of F.

39
00:02:01,616 --> 00:02:02,625
Now the rain.

40
00:02:02,625 --> 00:02:06,902
Rain and no rain are mutually exclusive,
and together they make the whole

41
00:02:06,902 --> 00:02:07,855
sample space.

42
00:02:08,605 --> 00:02:11,994
So P of rain, plus P
of no rain, equals one.

43
00:02:11,994 --> 00:02:14,400
Seventy percent plus thirty percent.

44
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:17,564
That is why no rain is
exactly thirty percent.

45
00:02:18,314 --> 00:02:22,892
And when every outcome is equally
likely, like the faces of a fair die,

46
00:02:22,892 --> 00:02:27,579
the probability of E is the number of
outcomes in E, divided by the number

47
00:02:27,579 --> 00:02:28,479
in S.

48
00:02:28,841 --> 00:02:29,957
Side by side.

49
00:02:29,957 --> 00:02:32,408
Even and odd never share a number.

50
00:02:32,408 --> 00:02:36,803
So P of even or odd is one half
plus one half, which is one.

51
00:02:37,203 --> 00:02:40,767
Even and more than three do
share numbers: four and six.

52
00:02:40,767 --> 00:02:44,659
Adding would count them twice,
so we subtract the overlap once.

53
00:02:45,259 --> 00:02:46,159
Your turn.

54
00:02:46,102 --> 00:02:51,034
On one throw, are getting a two and
getting an even number mutually exclusive?

55
00:02:51,034 --> 00:02:52,226
Pause and think.

56
00:02:55,426 --> 00:02:56,326
No!

57
00:02:55,853 --> 00:03:00,132
A two is also an even number, so
both can happen on the same throw.

58
00:03:00,782 --> 00:03:01,711
Remember this.

59
00:03:01,711 --> 00:03:05,686
Probabilities are never negative,
they add up to one, and an overlap is

60
00:03:05,686 --> 00:03:06,586
counted once.

61
00:03:07,271 --> 00:03:08,961
Now, one worked example.

62
00:03:09,311 --> 00:03:10,637
A die is thrown.

63
00:03:10,637 --> 00:03:12,231
A is an even number.

64
00:03:12,231 --> 00:03:14,461
B is a number more than three.

65
00:03:14,461 --> 00:03:16,744
Find the probability of A or B.

66
00:03:17,244 --> 00:03:18,144
Step one.

67
00:03:17,987 --> 00:03:21,239
The sample space has six
outcomes, all equally likely.

68
00:03:21,989 --> 00:03:22,889
Step two.

69
00:03:22,853 --> 00:03:24,811
A is two, four and six.

70
00:03:24,811 --> 00:03:26,823
B is four, five and six.

71
00:03:27,573 --> 00:03:28,473
Step three.

72
00:03:28,473 --> 00:03:30,489
They overlap in four and six.

73
00:03:30,489 --> 00:03:33,220
So A and B has probability two over six.

74
00:03:33,970 --> 00:03:34,870
Step four.

75
00:03:34,774 --> 00:03:38,973
P of A or B is three sixths, plus
three sixths, minus two sixths.

76
00:03:39,723 --> 00:03:42,167
That is four sixths, or two thirds.

77
00:03:42,167 --> 00:03:45,271
Check: A or B is two, four, five and six.

78
00:03:45,271 --> 00:03:46,522
Four out of six.

79
00:03:47,172 --> 00:03:48,634
Now, the exam trap.

80
00:03:48,984 --> 00:03:50,524
Many students just add.

81
00:03:50,524 --> 00:03:54,836
Three sixths plus three sixths gives
one, as if A or B were certain.

82
00:03:55,236 --> 00:03:59,690
But a throw of one is in neither
A nor B, so it cannot be certain.

83
00:03:59,690 --> 00:04:01,825
Four and six were counted twice.

84
00:04:01,825 --> 00:04:05,447
Subtract the overlap once,
and the answer is two thirds.

85
00:04:06,097 --> 00:04:07,444
Where is this asked?

86
00:04:07,444 --> 00:04:08,375
In JEE Main.

87
00:04:08,375 --> 00:04:12,568
In January twenty twenty, a paper gave
the chance that exactly one of two

88
00:04:12,568 --> 00:04:16,707
events happens, and the chance that
at least one happens, and asked for

89
00:04:16,707 --> 00:04:18,008
the chance of both.

90
00:04:18,608 --> 00:04:19,839
Exam level now.

91
00:04:19,839 --> 00:04:23,542
Find the probability that
A happens, but B does not.

92
00:04:23,542 --> 00:04:24,826
Pause and solve.

93
00:04:30,226 --> 00:04:31,655
The answer is B.

94
00:04:31,655 --> 00:04:33,073
Zero point three.

95
00:04:33,573 --> 00:04:35,306
First, find the overlap.

96
00:04:35,306 --> 00:04:39,507
P of A and B is P of A, plus
P of B, minus P of A or B.

97
00:04:40,007 --> 00:04:45,103
That is zero point six, plus zero point
five, minus zero point eight, which

98
00:04:45,103 --> 00:04:46,640
is zero point three.

99
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:50,014
A but not B is A, minus the overlap.

100
00:04:50,014 --> 00:04:54,083
Zero point six minus zero point
three is zero point three.

101
00:04:54,683 --> 00:04:56,223
Here is your recap.

102
00:04:56,223 --> 00:04:57,806
Screenshot this card.

103
00:05:01,006 --> 00:05:03,192
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

104
00:05:03,592 --> 00:05:04,492
One.

105
00:05:04,044 --> 00:05:06,454
What are the three axioms of probability?

106
00:05:07,354 --> 00:05:08,254
Two.

107
00:05:07,850 --> 00:05:11,391
If P of E is zero point
four, what is P of not E?

108
00:05:12,291 --> 00:05:13,191
Three.

109
00:05:12,778 --> 00:05:15,204
Why can a probability
never be more than one?

110
00:05:16,304 --> 00:05:18,492
That completes Class eleven maths.

111
00:05:18,492 --> 00:05:22,042
Every episode is in the PUC
Maths Class eleven playlist.

112
00:05:22,042 --> 00:05:23,743
See you in Class twelve.
